Complete Flow: Git Clone → Docker Deployment → Debugging
Step 1: Clone the repository
Command
git clone <repository-url>

Example:
git clone https://github.com/company/devops-exam-portal.git

What happens?
Git downloads:
devops-exam-portal/
│
├── backend/
├── frontend/
├── docker-compose.yml
├── README.md
└── .env.example

First check after cloning
Go inside:
cd devops-exam-portal

Check files:
ls -la

Expected:
backend
frontend
docker-compose.yml
.env.example

Step 2: Understand project structure
Before running anything, identify components.
Your project:

devops-exam-portal

├── backend
│   ├── server.js
│   ├── routes/
│   ├── models/
│   └── config/
│
├── frontend
│   ├── src/
│   ├── nginx.conf
│   └── Dockerfile
│
├── docker-compose.yml
└── .env

Architecture:
User Browser
      |
      |
      v
Frontend (React + Nginx)
Port 3000
      |
      |
      v
Backend (Node Express)
Port 5001
      |
      |
      v
MySQL
Port 3306

Step 3: Check Docker installation
Before deployment:
docker --version

Example:
Docker version 28.x

Check Compose:
docker compose version

If you get:
docker: 'compose' is not a docker command

then Docker Compose plugin is missing.
Step 4: Check Docker Compose file
Open:
cat docker-compose.yml

Look for services:
Example:

services:

 mysql:
   image: mysql:8.0

 backend:
   build: ./backend

 frontend:
   build: ./frontend

This tells you:
Three containers will run:

mysql
backend
frontend

Step 5: Configure environment variables
Check:
ls -la

If .env missing:
.env not found

Create:
cp .env.example .env

Edit:
nano .env

Example:
MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD=root123

DB_NAME=devops_exam_db

DB_USER=exam_user

DB_PASSWORD=password

PORT=5001

Why?
Docker Compose reads:

${DB_NAME}

from:
.env

Step 6: Build and start containers
Command:
docker compose up -d --build

Meaning:
up
=
create containers

-d
=
run in background

--build
=
create images again

Docker will:
Pull images
Example:
mysql:8.0

Build your images:
devops-exam-portal-backend
devops-exam-portal-frontend

Start containers
Step 7: First debugging point - Build errors
Example:
You got:

compose build requires buildx 0.17.0 or later

Debug:
Check:

docker buildx version

Why?
Docker Compose v2 uses BuildKit.

Fix:

Update Docker/Buildx.

Step 8: Check container status
Always run:
docker compose ps

Expected:
NAME              STATUS

backend           Up
frontend          Up
mysql             Up (healthy)

If you see:
Exited

something failed.
Step 9: Check logs
Never guess. Check logs.
Backend:

docker compose logs backend

You got:
Database connected.
Server running on port 5001

Meaning:
✅ Node started
✅ Database connected

Frontend:
docker compose logs frontend

Check:
nginx started

Meaning:
Frontend server works.

Step 10: Test backend directly
Before testing UI:
Test API.

Your backend had:

app.get('/health')

So:
curl http://localhost:5001/health

Response:
{
 "status":"ok"
}

Meaning:
Backend is alive.

Step 11: Test frontend
From EC2:
curl http://localhost:3000

Expected:
<!doctype html>
<html>

Meaning:
Frontend works.

Step 12: User login failed debugging
Problem:
Login failed

Do not immediately change code.
Follow the request path:

Browser
 |
 |
Frontend
 |
 |
Backend
 |
 |
Database

Find where it breaks.
Check backend logs
Command:
docker compose logs -f backend

Result:
Database connected.
Server running

No login request.
Conclusion:

Request never reached backend

Problem is before backend.
Step 13: Check frontend API configuration
Search:
grep -R "api\|5001\|localhost" frontend/

Found:
frontend/nginx.conf

with:
proxy_pass http://backend:5000/api/

Problem:
Frontend sends:

frontend
   |
   |
backend:5000

But backend runs:
backend:5001

Step 14: Confirm with logs
Frontend logs:
docker compose logs frontend

Found:
POST /api/auth/login

upstream:
backend:5000

connection refused

This confirms:
Wrong backend port

Step 15: Fix configuration
Change:
proxy_pass http://backend:5000/api/;

to:
proxy_pass http://backend:5001/api/;

Rebuild:
docker compose up -d --build frontend

Step 16: Verify database data
Enter MySQL:
docker compose exec mysql mysql -u root -p

Show databases:
SHOW DATABASES;

Select:
USE devops_exam_db;

Tables:
SHOW TABLES;

Check results:
SELECT * FROM results;

Common Debug Commands Cheat Sheet
Containers
docker ps
docker compose ps

Logs
docker compose logs backend

docker compose logs frontend

docker compose logs -f backend

Enter container
Backend:
docker compose exec backend bash

MySQL:
docker compose exec mysql mysql -u root -p

Check ports
netstat -tulpn

or:
docker port container_name

Check environment
docker compose exec backend env

Check API
curl http://localhost:5001/health

Real DevOps Debugging Mindset
When something fails, follow this order:
1. Code exists?
        |
        v
2. Configuration correct?
        |
        v
3. Docker image built?
        |
        v
4. Container running?
        |
        v
5. Application logs?
        |
        v
6. Network connection?
        |
        v
7. Database connection?
        |
        v
8. User workflow test?

In your case:
Git clone
    ↓
Docker Compose issue
    ↓
.env missing
    ↓
Buildx issue
    ↓
Containers started
    ↓
Backend health checked
    ↓
Frontend login failed
    ↓
Nginx proxy debugging
    ↓
Wrong backend port found
    ↓
Fixed and rebuilt
    ↓
Database verification

This is the exact troubleshooting pattern used in real DevOps production environments.
